IC=ref('ESE Clinical Practice Guideline: Endocrine Adverse Conditions During Checkpoint Inhibition, 2022','https://www.bijniernet.nl/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/EJE-2022-187-6-Endocrine-related-adverse-conditions-in-patients-receiving-immune-checkpoint-inhibition-an-ESE-clinical-practice-guideline.pdf')
q('A patient with a pituitary macroadenoma has low free T4 with TSH 2.0 mIU/L. Which interpretation is most appropriate?', 'Central hypothyroidism with an inappropriately normal TSH',[
 ('Normal thyroid function because TSH is within range','A normal TSH is inappropriate when free T4 is low in pituitary disease.'),('Primary hypothyroidism is established by this TSH','Primary thyroid failure usually raises TSH appropriately.'),('TSH alone excludes any need for thyroid assessment','Central disease requires free T4 interpretation.')],
 'A pituitary hormone must be judged against its target-gland response', '**Central hypothyroidism can have low, normal or mildly elevated immunoreactive TSH.**',['The TSH may not have normal biological activity.','Severe nonthyroidal illness and drugs can complicate interpretation.'], '**Assess the other axes before replacement.**',['Adrenal safety is especially important.','Use free T4 and the clinical context rather than a screening-TSH shortcut.'],[['Free T4','Low'],['TSH','Inappropriately non-elevated'],['Context','Pituitary disease']],[HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('Which laboratory marker primarily guides levothyroxine adjustment in central hypothyroidism?', 'Free T4 rather than normalization of TSH',[
 ('TSH alone as in uncomplicated primary hypothyroidism','TSH is not a reliable dosing target in central disease.'),('Serum prolactin as the thyroid-dose marker','It does not measure thyroid replacement adequacy.'),('Morning cortisol as the sole thyroid-dose target','Cortisol assesses a different axis.')],
 'The failed controller cannot serve as the usual feedback marker', '**An appropriate free-T4 target is commonly in the upper half of the reference range, individualized to the patient.**',['Age, cardiac disease, symptoms and assay timing matter.','A low TSH during adequate replacement does not automatically mean overtreatment.'], '**Assess adherence, interactions and blood-draw timing.**',['Avoid increasing the dose from TSH alone.','Reassess when estrogen, GH or other relevant therapies change.'],[['Primary hypothyroidism','TSH usually guides'],['Central hypothyroidism','Free T4 guides'],['Target','Individualized appropriate free T4']],[HP])
q('Both central hypothyroidism and possible ACTH deficiency are found. What is the safest replacement sequence?', 'Assess and treat adrenal insufficiency before or with starting thyroid hormone',[
 ('Start high-dose levothyroxine first and ignore cortisol','This can precipitate adrenal decompensation.'),('Replace thyroid hormone only if TSH becomes high','Central disease does not require a high TSH.'),('Withhold all replacement indefinitely','Potentially dangerous deficiencies need timely treatment.')],
 'Thyroid replacement can increase cortisol requirements and clearance', '**Unrecognized adrenal insufficiency can become clinically apparent or worsen after levothyroxine.**',['If urgent replacement is needed before definitive testing, glucocorticoid coverage may be appropriate.','The precise plan depends on illness severity.'], '**Secure adrenal safety and then optimize thyroid treatment.**',['Teach glucocorticoid stress dosing when deficiency is confirmed.','Do not use the sequence rule to delay emergency treatment.'],[['First concern','Cortisol adequacy'],['Then','Appropriate thyroid replacement'],['Risk if reversed','Adrenal crisis']],[HP])
q('An 8 AM cortisol is 7 µg/dL in a stable patient with suspected hypopituitarism. What is the usual next diagnostic principle?', 'Perform an appropriate dynamic adrenal assessment, using assay-specific interpretation',[
 ('The value definitively excludes central adrenal insufficiency','It lies in the indeterminate range in the guideline framework.'),('A random evening cortisol is always a better standalone test','Morning timing and appropriate dynamic evaluation are preferred.'),('A single universal stimulated cutoff applies to every modern assay','Assay methods change the appropriate threshold.')],
 'Intermediate morning values do not settle adrenal reserve', '**The 2016 guideline suggests dynamic testing when morning cortisol is between 3 and 15 µg/dL.**',['Values outside the range also need clinical and assay context.','Recent glucocorticoids and binding-protein changes can interfere.'], '**Use a test suitable for the timing and suspected lesion.**',['Do not delay crisis treatment for elective testing.','Interpret a result with the pretest probability.'],[['Morning result','Intermediate'],['Next step','Dynamic assessment'],['Cutoff','Assay and context dependent']],[HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('Why can ACTH-deficient adrenal insufficiency present with normal potassium?', 'Aldosterone secretion is largely preserved through renin-angiotensin control',[
 ('Central adrenal failure always causes complete aldosterone loss','Mineralocorticoid control is relatively independent of ACTH.'),('A normal potassium excludes any cortisol deficiency','It does not.'),('ACTH deficiency necessarily increases aldosterone enough to cause hypokalemia','This is not its defining mechanism.')],
 'The two adrenal hormone systems have different main controllers', '**Cortisol depends on ACTH, whereas aldosterone mainly responds to angiotensin II and potassium.**',['Hyponatremia can still occur through impaired water excretion.','Hyperpigmentation is not typical of central deficiency.'], '**Do not use the absence of hyperkalemia to dismiss the diagnosis.**',['Glucocorticoid replacement is needed when confirmed.','Routine fludrocortisone is usually unnecessary in central disease.'],[['Central deficiency','Low ACTH/cortisol'],['Aldosterone','Often preserved'],['Potassium','Can remain normal']],[HP])
q('A patient has a new pituitary injury and strong clinical concern for ACTH deficiency, but an early cosyntropin test is normal. What limitation matters?', 'The adrenal glands may still respond before chronic ACTH deprivation has caused atrophy',[
 ('An early normal response excludes every recent central lesion','The timing can produce a falsely reassuring result.'),('Cosyntropin directly measures hypothalamic CRH secretion','It tests adrenal response to ACTH stimulation.'),('All future reassessment is unnecessary','Reassessment may be needed as the axis evolves.')],
 'Test sensitivity depends on disease timing', '**Recent central adrenal failure may precede loss of adrenal responsiveness.**',['Other dynamic tests or repeat assessment can be appropriate.','Safety and contraindications determine test selection.'], '**Use clinical judgment and protective treatment when indicated.**',['Do not ignore evolving symptoms because of one early test.','Arrange a clear follow-up plan.'],[['Recent injury','Adrenal responsiveness can persist'],['Chronic deprivation','Response may decline'],['Interpretation','Timing and clinical probability']],[HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('A man with a sellar mass has low morning testosterone and inappropriately normal LH and FSH. Which pattern is present?', 'Central hypogonadism',[
 ('Primary testicular failure as the best fit','That usually raises gonadotropins.'),('Normal gonadal function because LH is within range','LH is inappropriate for the low testosterone.'),('Autonomous gonadotropin excess','The target hormone is low and gonadotropins are not appropriately increased.')],
 'Interpret target hormone and trophic hormone together', '**Pituitary or hypothalamic dysfunction can reduce gonadotropin drive.**',['Prolactin excess, drugs, obesity and illness are important additional considerations.','Repeat appropriately timed testosterone assessment may be needed.'], '**Address the cause and reproductive goals.**',['Testosterone replacement does not restore fertility in the same way as gonadotropin therapy.','Review contraindications and the other pituitary axes.'],[['Central pattern','Low testosterone with low/inappropriate LH/FSH'],['Primary pattern','Low testosterone with high gonadotropins'],['Treatment choice','Depends on fertility and cause']],[HP,PR],'clinical_scenario')
q('How is suspected adult GH deficiency usually confirmed when the diagnosis is not already established by a highly specific clinical context?', 'An appropriate stimulation test rather than a single random GH concentration',[
 ('Any low random GH proves deficiency','Normal secretion is pulsatile and low between pulses.'),('A normal IGF-1 excludes all adult GH deficiency','IGF-1 can be normal in some affected adults.'),('The same stimulation cutoff applies to every BMI and assay','Interpretation depends on test, assay and patient factors.')],
 'Low secretion between pulses is not itself pathological', '**Dynamic tests assess secretory reserve.**',['Insulin tolerance testing has important safety contraindications.','Other validated tests are chosen according to availability and suitability.'], '**Consider the overall pituitary phenotype.**',['Multiple established deficits and a structural cause alter diagnostic certainty.','Replacement requires individualized benefit-risk review and monitoring.'],[['Random GH','Poor standalone test'],['Dynamic testing','Assesses reserve'],['Interpretation','Protocol, BMI and assay dependent']],[HP])
q('After a major postpartum hemorrhage, a woman cannot lactate and later develops amenorrhea, fatigue and low free T4 with non-elevated TSH. Which diagnosis is most likely?', 'Sheehan syndrome',[
 ('Postpartum thyroiditis alone','It does not explain the combined pituitary hormone deficits and lactation failure after shock.'),('Primary ovarian insufficiency alone','It does not explain lactation and thyroid-axis abnormalities.'),('An isolated prolactinoma','Prolactin deficiency rather than typical excess is suggested.')],
 'The enlarged pregnancy pituitary is vulnerable to ischemic injury', '**Severe peripartum circulatory compromise can damage anterior pituitary function.**',['Recognition can be delayed.','ACTH deficiency may be the most immediately dangerous component.'], '**Assess and replace all relevant axes safely.**',['Provide glucocorticoid coverage before thyroid replacement when needed.','Long-term imaging can show an atrophic gland or empty sella.'],[['Trigger','Major postpartum hemorrhage/shock'],['Early clue','Failure to lactate'],['Later pattern','Multiple pituitary deficits']],[HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient receiving a checkpoint inhibitor develops headache, fatigue, low cortisol and low ACTH. MRI is not markedly enlarged. What is the key interpretation?', 'Immune-related pituitary dysfunction can occur without dramatic MRI enlargement',[
 ('A normal-looking MRI excludes central adrenal insufficiency','Functional immune-related injury may have minimal imaging findings.'),('Checkpoint therapy causes only thyroid disease','Pituitary and adrenal-axis toxicity also occur.'),('Wait for all test results before treating suspected adrenal crisis','Urgent glucocorticoid treatment must not be delayed in an unstable patient.')],
 'Functional deficits can be more important than gland size', '**Checkpoint-associated hypophysitis or isolated ACTH deficiency can cause dangerous cortisol deficiency.**',['Patterns vary by immunotherapy class.','Other causes of low cortisol still require consideration.'], '**Treat hormone deficits and coordinate oncology/endocrine care.**',['High-dose anti-inflammatory steroids are not automatically required for every stable isolated hormonal deficit.','Mass-effect symptoms and severity change management.'],[['Exposure','Checkpoint inhibitor'],['Pattern','Possible ACTH deficiency'],['MRI','May be minimally abnormal']],[HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('Sudden severe headache, vomiting, ophthalmoplegia and visual deterioration occur in a patient with a sellar mass. Which diagnosis must be considered urgently?', 'Pituitary apoplexy',[
 ('Uncomplicated chronic migraine as the only diagnosis','The visual and cranial-nerve findings with a sellar lesion require urgent assessment.'),('A routine stable microincidentaloma requiring annual review only','This is an acute neurological/endocrine emergency.'),('Isolated medication-induced prolactin elevation','This does not explain the acute compressive syndrome.')],
 'Hemorrhage or infarction in a pituitary lesion can threaten vision and adrenal function', '**Apoplexy can mimic subarachnoid hemorrhage or meningitis.**',['Acute ACTH loss can cause hemodynamic collapse.','MRI is the preferred diagnostic imaging when feasible.'], '**Stabilize and obtain urgent multidisciplinary review.**',['Assess visual fields, acuity, consciousness and circulation.','Do not overlook cortisol replacement while arranging imaging.'],[['Presentation','Acute headache plus ocular/visual signs'],['Mechanism','Pituitary hemorrhage or infarction'],['Danger','Vision and adrenal crisis']],[AP],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with suspected pituitary apoplexy is hypotensive and drowsy. What is the immediate endocrine priority?', 'Give stress-dose hydrocortisone promptly while stabilizing circulation',[
 ('Wait for dynamic adrenal testing before giving steroids','This can delay lifesaving treatment.'),('Start levothyroxine first without adrenal cover','This can worsen unrecognized cortisol deficiency.'),('Treat only the headache and defer endocrine assessment','Acute adrenal failure can be fatal.')],
 'Emergency replacement takes priority over perfect diagnostic sampling', '**If feasible, draw cortisol and other samples before steroids without delaying care.**',['Use the adrenal-crisis protocol and appropriate fluids.','Severe or worsening visual or consciousness impairment influences surgical assessment.'], '**Arrange urgent endocrine, neurosurgical and ophthalmic input.**',['Not every apoplexy requires identical immediate surgery.','Reassess pituitary and visual function during recovery.'],[['Unstable patient','Prompt hydrocortisone'],['Samples','Before treatment only if no delay'],['Surgery','Clinical severity and expert decision']],[AP],'clinical_scenario')
q('ACTH-dependent hypercortisolism is biochemically established, but MRI shows only an equivocal 3-mm pituitary focus. What investigation can help distinguish a pituitary from an ectopic ACTH source?', 'Bilateral inferior petrosal sinus sampling in an experienced center',[
 ('Assume every tiny MRI focus is a corticotroph tumor','Small incidental lesions are common enough to mislead.'),('Use IPSS to establish hypercortisolism before any biochemical diagnosis','IPSS is a source-localization test, not the initial hypercortisolism test.'),('A low-dose dexamethasone test alone always localizes the source','It does not reliably distinguish pituitary from ectopic secretion.')],
 'A small image finding must be linked to the hormone mechanism', '**IPSS assesses central-to-peripheral ACTH gradients under a validated protocol.**',['Active hypercortisolism should be confirmed at the relevant time.','Technical and biological false results remain possible.'], '**Use the test for a defined unresolved localization question.**',['Do not rely on its lateralization as an infallible surgical map.','Integrate imaging, dynamic data and expert interpretation.'],[['Prerequisite','Confirmed ACTH-dependent disease'],['Question','Pituitary versus ectopic source'],['Setting','Experienced sampling center']],[CU,IP],'clinical_scenario')
q('A patient with confirmed cortisol excess has consistently suppressed ACTH. What does this imply for pituitary source testing?', 'An ACTH-independent mechanism should be evaluated rather than routine IPSS',[
 ('Suppressed ACTH proves Cushing disease','Pituitary ACTH-driven disease should not have appropriately suppressed ACTH in the same way.'),('Every cortisol excess requires IPSS regardless of ACTH','Source testing follows the ACTH branch.'),('The result means cortisol excess is impossible','Autonomous adrenal cortisol can suppress ACTH.')],
 'Establish the dependency branch before localization', '**Cushing disease specifically denotes pituitary ACTH-driven cortisol excess.**',['ACTH-independent disease commonly directs adrenal evaluation.','Sampling and assay issues require attention when values are unexpected.'], '**Avoid incidental-image-driven diagnosis.**',['A small pituitary lesion can coexist with an unrelated adrenal cause.','Confirm the biochemical pattern before procedural testing.'],[['Low ACTH','Consider ACTH-independent disease'],['Nonsuppressed ACTH','ACTH-dependent pathway'],['IPSS','Selected dependent-source question']],[CU])
q('A calcified mixed cystic-solid suprasellar lesion causes visual symptoms, pituitary deficits and new AVP deficiency. Which diagnosis is particularly suggestive?', 'Craniopharyngioma',[
 ('An ordinary small prolactinoma as the best structural fit','The suprasellar cystic-calcified pattern and AVP involvement suggest another lesion.'),('An isolated adrenal adenoma','This cannot explain the local sellar syndrome.'),('A peripheral thyroid nodule','It does not account for the anatomical findings.')],
 'Posterior-pituitary and hypothalamic involvement broaden the sellar differential', '**Craniopharyngiomas can affect the stalk, optic pathways and hypothalamus.**',['Metastatic, inflammatory and germ-cell lesions are other considerations when AVP deficiency accompanies a mass.','Ordinary pituitary adenomas less commonly present with preoperative AVP deficiency.'], '**Plan specialist anatomical and hormonal care.**',['Assess cortisol and fluid balance promptly.','Long-term hypothalamic and metabolic complications matter in treatment selection.'],[['Location','Suprasellar/stalk region'],['Pattern','Cystic-solid, often calcified'],['Consequences','Vision, hormones and water balance']],[IN,HP],'clinical_scenario')
q('After pituitary surgery, early dilute polyuria is followed several days later by hyponatremia and concentrated urine. What physiological sequence can explain this?', 'An evolving postoperative AVP disturbance, including an antidiuretic phase after initial deficiency',[
 ('All postoperative polyuria is permanent nephrogenic AVP resistance','Central transient disturbances are common considerations.'),('Hyponatremia proves desmopressin must be increased automatically','Excess antidiuresis can worsen it.'),('Water balance can be assumed stable after the first normal sodium','Delayed changes can occur after discharge.')],
 'Postoperative water disorders can change direction', '**The classic triphasic response can include deficiency, inappropriate antidiuresis and later recurrent deficiency.**',['Not every patient develops all phases.','Desmopressin exposure and cortisol deficiency can also affect sodium.'], '**Use serial sodium, urine output and clinical assessment.**',['Adjust hormone and fluid plans to the current phase.','Provide discharge instructions for delayed hyponatremic symptoms.'],[['Early phase','Possible dilute polyuria'],['Later phase','Possible water retention/hyponatremia'],['Safety','Reassess rather than repeat a fixed plan']],[HP],'clinical_scenario')

